1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:03,000
A cable under the Atlantic.

2
00:00:03,001 --> 00:00:08,000
The dream of instantaneous communication between the new world and the old.

3
00:00:08,001 --> 00:00:12,000
In its day, equivalent to landing a man on the moon.

4
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:15,000
Its creator, a man once called the greatest American.

5
00:00:15,001 --> 00:00:18,000
A man most Americans today cannot name.

6
00:00:18,001 --> 00:00:24,000
Now, the amazing voyage of the transatlantic cable on modern marvels.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:40,640
Mid-Pacific, 1996.

8
00:00:42,060 --> 00:00:47,120
A ship pays out a tiny one-inch diameter fiber-optic submarine cable.

9
00:00:47,940 --> 00:00:52,220
This single cable will connect 75% of the world's population.

10
00:00:52,960 --> 00:00:57,360
To the average modern telephone user, submarine cables mean little.

11
00:00:57,361 --> 00:01:04,360
yet they are one of the single most important links to international communications.

12
00:01:06,360 --> 00:01:13,360
Even in the space age, modern fiber optic cables transmit voice and data traffic with much higher reliability

13
00:01:13,361 --> 00:01:16,360
and at a cheaper rate than satellite transmissions do.

14
00:01:16,361 --> 00:01:22,360
With over 320 active submarine cables lying on our ocean floors,

15
00:01:22,361 --> 00:01:26,880
carrying over two billion minutes of voice and data traffic per year.

16
00:01:27,760 --> 00:01:31,960
Submarine cables have become the mainstay of our global village.

17
00:01:35,060 --> 00:01:40,900
But this ease and speed of contact that underwater cables make possible is relatively new.

18
00:01:41,480 --> 00:01:45,680
A little over 150 years ago, there were no submarine cables at all,

19
00:01:45,960 --> 00:01:48,220
let alone any that link continents together.

20
00:01:52,360 --> 00:02:00,080
When the telegraph was invented in 1837,

21
00:02:00,640 --> 00:02:05,180
engineers dreamed of communicating across oceans, lakes, and rivers.

22
00:02:06,800 --> 00:02:11,340
It was the inability to waterproof, pressurize, and insulate a cable

23
00:02:11,341 --> 00:02:13,200
from the ocean's conductive medium

24
00:02:13,201 --> 00:02:17,420
that stalled for years any progress of telegraphy through the water.

25
00:02:18,520 --> 00:02:20,180
It just so happens that in the 1840s,

26
00:02:20,181 --> 00:02:24,180
something that had been known for a long time in Southeast Asia and Malaya,

27
00:02:24,181 --> 00:02:28,180
was made available in Europe. It's called gutta percha.

28
00:02:28,181 --> 00:02:32,180
It's sort of like rubber and it's a sap from a tree, but instead of being

29
00:02:32,181 --> 00:02:36,180
stretchy like rubber, it's really a plastic.

30
00:02:36,181 --> 00:02:40,180
It's very good insulator and it's not bothered by pressure

31
00:02:40,181 --> 00:02:44,180
or water. So it became the ideal insulin for

32
00:02:44,181 --> 00:02:48,180
telegraph cables.

33
00:02:48,181 --> 00:02:56,180
One of the first successful Gutter-percha submarine telegraph cables to be installed was across New York's Hudson River in 1848.

34
00:02:58,180 --> 00:03:06,180
By 1852, submarine cables up to 300 miles long were connecting England, Holland, Germany, Denmark and Sweden.

35
00:03:07,180 --> 00:03:11,180
Others were even connecting Italy with Corsica and Sardinia.

36
00:03:11,181 --> 00:03:18,680
But something as grand as stringing a 3,000 mile cable across the Atlantic Ocean

37
00:03:18,681 --> 00:03:21,240
was still nothing more than a dream.

38
00:03:30,720 --> 00:03:37,380
Cyrus Westfield is considered by many historians as one of the greatest Americans of the 19th century.

39
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:39,960
Yet there are few who remember him.

40
00:03:40,580 --> 00:03:45,680
This man won no wars, cured no illnesses, saved no lives.

41
00:03:46,120 --> 00:03:50,960
His greatest accomplishment lies buried in sludge at the bottom of the Atlantic Ocean

42
00:03:50,961 --> 00:03:53,560
and has for 150 years.

43
00:03:55,080 --> 00:03:57,420
Field was born in 1819.

44
00:03:58,200 --> 00:04:02,780
He was a sick child, and he spent his first several years in bed,

45
00:04:02,781 --> 00:04:08,600
and the rest of the family always said he was making up for lost time during the rest of his life.

46
00:04:08,601 --> 00:04:20,680
He was described as a squirmy young man, always starting projects, always extremely busy, never an idle moment.

47
00:04:22,880 --> 00:04:27,680
At the age of 15, Field went to work for a dry goods store in New York City.

48
00:04:28,360 --> 00:04:34,520
It was there he learned the fundamentals of running a business and the art of negotiating financial transactions.

49
00:04:35,680 --> 00:04:37,840
Field wouldn't last long as a store clerk.

50
00:04:38,600 --> 00:04:43,180
By the age of 18, he had created his own papermaking company

51
00:04:43,181 --> 00:04:47,720
to satisfy a growing demand for high-quality stationary products.

52
00:04:48,160 --> 00:04:50,220
Cyrus Field grew enormously wealthy.

53
00:04:50,820 --> 00:04:52,340
He drove himself relentlessly.

54
00:04:53,040 --> 00:04:56,220
He was up sometimes nearly 24 hours a day working.

55
00:04:56,980 --> 00:04:59,040
Eventually, this caused his health to fail.

56
00:05:00,640 --> 00:05:03,180
Virtually exhausted and under doctor's orders,

57
00:05:03,181 --> 00:05:06,020
Field was forced to retire at the age of 33.

58
00:05:06,021 --> 00:05:11,960
He was planning to spend the rest of his life leisurely traveling around the world with his wife.

59
00:05:13,920 --> 00:05:19,900
However, in January of 1854, one late night meeting changed Field's life forever.

60
00:05:20,900 --> 00:05:24,040
He met with an English engineer named Frederick Gisborne,

61
00:05:24,340 --> 00:05:27,880
who had been in charge of building a telegraph line across Newfoundland.

62
00:05:29,120 --> 00:05:31,820
It was an extremely important project.

63
00:05:31,821 --> 00:05:36,880
It would decrease by several days the length of time it took for news to cross the Atlantic.

64
00:05:37,880 --> 00:05:40,078
Steamers from Europe could arrive in St.

65
00:05:40,079 --> 00:05:42,880
John's and flash urgent messages ahead to New York.

66
00:05:46,640 --> 00:05:50,520
Unfortunately, the costs far exceeded the original budget,

67
00:05:50,980 --> 00:05:54,020
and the Newfoundland Electric Telegraph Company went bankrupt,

68
00:05:54,320 --> 00:05:56,660
leaving Gisborne with a substantial debt.

69
00:05:56,661 --> 00:06:03,840
Gisborne met with Field in hopes of raising more capital to finish this important project

70
00:06:03,841 --> 00:06:11,160
Field, relaxing after a vacation to South America, had no intentions of investing in any new business ventures

71
00:06:11,161 --> 00:06:14,100
He politely listened, but promised nothing

72
00:06:14,101 --> 00:06:18,100
Later that evening, while alone in his library

73
00:06:18,101 --> 00:06:21,380
Cyrus examined his globe and suddenly realized

74
00:06:21,381 --> 00:06:26,460
that the Newfoundland Telegraph would be just one link in a far more important project

75
00:06:27,060 --> 00:06:29,860
Why wait for steamers to bring news from Europe?

76
00:06:30,380 --> 00:06:32,860
Make the telegraph do the entire job.

77
00:06:33,380 --> 00:06:36,480
Well, I mean, that's a sort of simple-minded approach

78
00:06:36,481 --> 00:06:40,260
that any of us would take who had no knowledge whatsoever of the technology.

79
00:06:40,540 --> 00:06:44,180
I mean, he didn't have the slightest idea of what the problems were,

80
00:06:44,860 --> 00:06:47,500
whether this was technologically feasible or not.

81
00:06:47,501 --> 00:06:51,160
So he contacts two people at first.

82
00:06:51,161 --> 00:07:00,940
First, Field contacted Lieutenant Matthew Morey, a prominent oceanographer and head of the National Observatory in Washington.

83
00:07:01,620 --> 00:07:03,029
His timing was perfect.

84
00:07:03,030 --> 00:07:07,500
Morey had just completed a series of soundings across the North Atlantic.

85
00:07:08,460 --> 00:07:17,360
Lieutenant Morey told Field, coincidentally, that he and his team of scientists had just recently discovered a plateau which ran all the way across the North Atlantic.

86
00:07:17,361 --> 00:07:24,360
The plateau was relatively smooth and soft and would be ideal for laying a cable across.

87
00:07:24,361 --> 00:07:32,360
It had a soft, mucky sediment on the top, and the cable could sink gently into that sediment.

88
00:07:32,361 --> 00:07:40,360
This comparatively narrow and flat area between America and Europe is exaggerated here for clarity.

89
00:07:41,360 --> 00:07:43,700
Its presence was great news to feel.

90
00:07:44,340 --> 00:07:46,940
The Atlantic Ocean has much deeper sections

91
00:07:46,941 --> 00:07:49,160
which would have made cable laying impossible

92
00:07:49,161 --> 00:07:51,860
due to pressures incurred at such depths,

93
00:07:51,861 --> 00:07:54,660
not to mention the many more miles of wire

94
00:07:54,661 --> 00:07:58,320
that would have been needed to slink up and down the ocean's mountains.

95
00:08:00,740 --> 00:08:03,100
Next field contact is Samuel Morris,

96
00:08:03,240 --> 00:08:06,840
the great pioneer in telecommunications in the 19th century.

97
00:08:07,540 --> 00:08:10,180
It was, of course, Morris who not only perfected

98
00:08:10,181 --> 00:08:13,340
and built the first telegraph in the United States, 1844,

99
00:08:13,720 --> 00:08:18,040
but also developed the telegraphic code that bears his name, Morse Code.

100
00:08:19,140 --> 00:08:21,980
Morse responded promptly to Field's inquiry.

101
00:08:22,340 --> 00:08:28,280
He said yes, in his opinion, telegraph signals could be broadcast via wire across the Atlantic Ocean.

102
00:08:29,280 --> 00:08:34,160
Ironically, 11 years earlier, Professor Morse had prophesied Field's dream.

103
00:08:34,680 --> 00:08:38,220
With the endorsement of Mori and the support of Morse,

104
00:08:38,221 --> 00:08:42,540
Field became obsessed with laying a transatlantic telegraph cable.

105
00:08:42,960 --> 00:08:46,180
He didn't know how, but he knew it would take lots of money.

106
00:08:46,840 --> 00:08:48,460
That would be the easy part.

107
00:08:49,180 --> 00:08:53,340
For what lay ahead were years of technical setbacks and disasters.

108
00:08:56,660 --> 00:09:00,000
From the time he had the idea for the transatlantic cable,

109
00:09:00,120 --> 00:09:04,580
it took Cyrus Field just three months to organize a company and raise the money.

110
00:09:05,560 --> 00:09:06,920
Modern Marvels will return.

111
00:09:06,921 --> 00:09:11,920
We now return to the transatlantic cable on Modern Marvels.

112
00:09:13,920 --> 00:09:18,920
Cyrus Field had no trouble finding backers for his transatlantic cable venture.

113
00:09:18,921 --> 00:09:24,920
It's amazing the number of wealthy friends that Field had who were eager to help him in the project.

114
00:09:24,921 --> 00:09:29,920
There was the great iron magnate and philanthropist Peter Cooper.

115
00:09:29,921 --> 00:09:35,920
There was Moses Taylor of banking fame and Marshall Roberts of the Erie Railroad.

116
00:09:35,921 --> 00:09:40,920
several of the industrial giants of America helped to form a company

117
00:09:40,921 --> 00:09:45,920
which became known as the New York, Newfoundland and London Telegraph Company.

118
00:09:47,920 --> 00:09:53,920
The company's first project was to finish the telegraph cable across the 400-mile width of Newfoundland,

119
00:09:53,921 --> 00:09:56,920
the easternmost part of North America.

120
00:09:56,921 --> 00:10:06,057
When completed in 1856, the company's telegraph network spanned nearly 1,000 miles from New York to St.

121
00:10:06,058 --> 00:10:07,920
John's, Newfoundland.

122
00:10:07,921 --> 00:10:11,920
Total cost for the project was well over a million dollars.

123
00:10:11,921 --> 00:10:20,920
Now one has to remember that a million dollars in those days, relative value of the dollar, means about 50 million dollars in today's dollars.

124
00:10:20,921 --> 00:10:25,920
The average Eastern working man put in 12 hours a day for a single dollar.

125
00:10:25,921 --> 00:10:29,700
So a million dollars was a serious outlay of capital.

126
00:10:30,440 --> 00:10:36,120
Unfortunately, completing the cable to Newfoundland was a lot bigger job than anyone anticipated,

127
00:10:36,740 --> 00:10:42,060
and they ended up using all of their resources just on that leg of the project.

128
00:10:42,900 --> 00:10:47,480
It was time to regroup and seek new investors

129
00:10:47,481 --> 00:10:53,200
and work on figuring out where they were going to get a ship to do it

130
00:10:53,201 --> 00:10:58,200
and where they were going to get a cable long enough to lay across the ocean floor.

131
00:11:07,140 --> 00:11:11,180
Though Field had many people convinced that his project was feasible,

132
00:11:11,640 --> 00:11:14,120
there were some who thought it was preposterous.

133
00:11:14,840 --> 00:11:18,580
Corporate giant Western Union spoke out strongly against Field's venture.

134
00:11:18,581 --> 00:11:23,580
They had their own grand plan to reach Europe by way of telegraph.

135
00:11:23,581 --> 00:11:31,580
Their route required only 56 miles of submarine cable across the Bering Strait via Alaska.

136
00:11:31,581 --> 00:11:36,580
Western Union had one outfit in Alaska and another outfit in Siberia.

137
00:11:36,581 --> 00:11:45,580
Their job was to gather poles for the telegraph wires and to negotiate with the natives for a right-of-way along the proposed route of the telegraph.

138
00:11:45,581 --> 00:11:46,281
telegraph.

139
00:11:46,282 --> 00:11:51,240
While they found plenty of timber in Alaska, they found almost no trees in Siberia.

140
00:11:51,900 --> 00:11:57,360
And this meant they had some almost insurmountable obstacles to overcome.

141
00:11:58,620 --> 00:12:04,280
But Western Union's view was that any route would be more feasible than something as risky

142
00:12:04,281 --> 00:12:09,071
as stretching a cable across 3,000 miles of water.

143
00:12:09,072 --> 00:12:12,520
Field ignored Western Union's logic.

144
00:12:12,521 --> 00:12:21,100
He was convinced the transatlantic route was possible and proceeded to lock down landing rights on both continents for his future wire.

145
00:12:21,940 --> 00:12:26,780
It was important to take the shortest route possible across the Atlantic because it's a long ways.

146
00:12:27,260 --> 00:12:32,580
They settled on a direct route from westernmost part of Ireland across to Newfoundland,

147
00:12:32,900 --> 00:12:36,520
which is the closest part of the North American continent to Europe.

148
00:12:36,521 --> 00:12:43,520
Now, with a mere 2,500 mile route established, Field was more confident than ever.

149
00:12:43,521 --> 00:12:48,520
He and his associates were able to get the British government to supply two ships,

150
00:12:48,521 --> 00:12:51,520
a small convoy, and money for the operation.

151
00:12:51,521 --> 00:12:57,520
This was an exchange for the transmission of government messages on the new cable, should it ever work.

152
00:13:00,520 --> 00:13:05,520
Unfortunately, in Field's homeland, America, it wasn't so easy.

153
00:13:05,521 --> 00:13:10,520
Western Union had begun work on its telegraph line through Alaska.

154
00:13:10,521 --> 00:13:20,520
Not many investors in the US would doubt the expertise of such an immensely powerful and profitable corporation that strongly opposed the transatlantic cable.

155
00:13:23,520 --> 00:13:27,520
Field pleaded with the US government for support in the form of naval ships.

156
00:13:27,521 --> 00:13:32,520
He needed them to make up half the convoy to get the wire across the ocean.

157
00:13:32,521 --> 00:13:39,360
The needed resolutions in Congress barely passed, and President Franklin Pierce signed

158
00:13:39,361 --> 00:13:46,400
them into law on his last day in office, March 3rd, 1857.

159
00:13:46,401 --> 00:13:51,160
Field's next priority was to begin manufacturing the submarine wire.

160
00:13:51,161 --> 00:13:54,900
Since cable laying in the North Atlantic was restricted to summer months due to weather

161
00:13:54,901 --> 00:13:58,040
conditions, he needed the cable immediately.

162
00:13:58,041 --> 00:14:09,520
Field, in his usual impatient manner, only gave Glass Elliot and company, the entity that was manufacturing the cable, only four months to produce the cable.

163
00:14:10,240 --> 00:14:14,480
As a result, the cable probably wasn't as good as it could have been.

164
00:14:15,260 --> 00:14:19,080
You can't just dump this wire in the water because it shores out.

165
00:14:19,360 --> 00:14:24,360
So what you do then is you coat it with some material called gutta percha.

166
00:14:24,361 --> 00:14:26,740
the gutter percha has virtually no strength

167
00:14:26,741 --> 00:14:28,220
and the copper doesn't have very much

168
00:14:28,221 --> 00:14:31,420
so you put iron wires around the outside

169
00:14:31,421 --> 00:14:33,800
you look inside

170
00:14:33,801 --> 00:14:34,700
and there's our

171
00:14:34,701 --> 00:14:36,760
copper wire in the middle

172
00:14:36,761 --> 00:14:38,560
gutter percha around it

173
00:14:38,561 --> 00:14:41,460
now we've got this protective wire on the outside

174
00:14:41,461 --> 00:14:47,020
in order to carry and pay out

175
00:14:47,021 --> 00:14:49,180
2,500 tons of this cable

176
00:14:49,181 --> 00:14:51,320
the two ships provided by the

177
00:14:51,321 --> 00:14:52,660
American and British governments

178
00:14:52,661 --> 00:14:55,880
had been extensively modified with circular tanks

179
00:14:55,881 --> 00:14:57,760
to store the massive wire.

180
00:14:59,100 --> 00:15:01,620
They loaded the cable onto two ships.

181
00:15:01,760 --> 00:15:04,940
It took 120 men three weeks to load

182
00:15:04,941 --> 00:15:09,780
the 2,000-some-odd miles of cable onto the two boats.

183
00:15:10,520 --> 00:15:13,100
The plan was to start off in Ireland

184
00:15:13,101 --> 00:15:15,700
with one of the ships laying the cable.

185
00:15:16,080 --> 00:15:20,480
At mid-ocean, they would splice the cable to the second ship,

186
00:15:20,481 --> 00:15:22,980
and the second ship would carry on to New Finland.

187
00:15:23,820 --> 00:15:26,820
On the morning of August 5th, 1857,

188
00:15:26,960 --> 00:15:28,760
with Cyrus Field on board,

189
00:15:29,360 --> 00:15:31,480
the cable was connected to the Irish shore

190
00:15:31,481 --> 00:15:33,700
with a huge celebratory send-off.

191
00:15:34,460 --> 00:15:36,840
To ensure that the cable remained intact,

192
00:15:37,200 --> 00:15:40,160
signals were continuously broadcast over the line

193
00:15:40,161 --> 00:15:41,300
from ship to shore.

194
00:15:42,900 --> 00:15:45,480
But only five miles were laid down

195
00:15:45,481 --> 00:15:48,520
when the cable got caught up in the ship's machinery and broke.

196
00:15:48,521 --> 00:15:51,700
This was quickly repaired and the mission continued.

197
00:15:52,200 --> 00:15:57,320
But then, 10 days and 300 miles into the mission, a real disaster struck.

198
00:15:57,940 --> 00:16:04,320
An anxious engineer, fearing that the cable was paying out too fast, suddenly applied the brakes.

199
00:16:05,140 --> 00:16:07,600
The brakes locked and the cable snapped.

200
00:16:08,400 --> 00:16:17,140
And the severed end of the cable, representing a half million dollars worth of cabled labor in time,

201
00:16:17,141 --> 00:16:22,440
25 million in today's dollars plunged into the sea and disappeared.

202
00:16:25,660 --> 00:16:29,820
A portion of Field's original cable was recently pulled from the ocean.

203
00:16:30,100 --> 00:16:33,620
Though over 100 years old, it could still conduct electricity.

204
00:16:34,280 --> 00:16:37,340
The transatlantic cable will continue on Modern Marvels.

205
00:16:38,300 --> 00:16:42,500
We now return to the transatlantic cable on Modern Marvels.

206
00:16:42,501 --> 00:16:52,660
The failure of the 1857 expedition made many skeptical of the ambitious project.

207
00:16:53,500 --> 00:16:56,060
Western Union's plan now appeared better than ever,

208
00:16:56,500 --> 00:16:59,220
as they continued to string their cable through Alaska.

209
00:16:59,880 --> 00:17:02,700
However, Cyrus Field remained optimistic.

210
00:17:03,820 --> 00:17:09,860
Although this unfortunate accident will postpone the completion of this undertaking for a short time,

211
00:17:09,861 --> 00:17:17,020
The result has been to convince all that took part in this experiment that with some slight alteration in the paying out machinery,

212
00:17:17,440 --> 00:17:21,140
there appears to be no great difficulty in laying down the cable.

213
00:17:21,600 --> 00:17:23,740
Cyrus Field, 1857.

214
00:17:25,780 --> 00:17:29,640
Field again raised more capital to launch a second attempt.

215
00:17:30,600 --> 00:17:34,800
The company ordered 700 miles of new wire to replace the lost cable.

216
00:17:35,640 --> 00:17:40,220
Engineers and scientists were hired to review every detail of the cable-laying procedure.

217
00:17:41,000 --> 00:17:46,320
They made many revisions to equipment on board and devised a new payout method,

218
00:17:46,321 --> 00:17:48,780
which included anti-lock brakes.

219
00:17:55,960 --> 00:18:01,560
It was decided for the expedition in 1858

220
00:18:01,561 --> 00:18:06,720
that instead of starting with one ship laying a cable in Ireland

221
00:18:06,721 --> 00:18:10,800
and then another ship picking up where that one left off in mid-ocean,

222
00:18:10,920 --> 00:18:14,760
they decided this time that the two ships would meet mid-ocean,

223
00:18:15,240 --> 00:18:16,560
splice the cable together.

224
00:18:17,000 --> 00:18:20,720
One would head for Ireland and another one would head for New Finland.

225
00:18:20,960 --> 00:18:23,340
That way it'd take half the time to lay the cable.

226
00:18:23,341 --> 00:18:27,740
And time was of the essence, given weather conditions and all.

227
00:18:27,741 --> 00:18:37,660
On June 10, 1858, both ships fully loaded and their convoy departed England for the middle of the Atlantic Ocean.

228
00:18:38,300 --> 00:18:46,520
There, under Cyrus Field's watchful eye, engineers spliced together the two cables, and the ships parted ways.

229
00:18:47,340 --> 00:18:51,660
Connected by the wire, they were able to communicate back and forth via telegraph.

230
00:18:51,661 --> 00:18:52,404
telegraph.

231
00:18:52,405 --> 00:18:58,280
But before the two ships even lost sight of each other, just three miles apart,

232
00:18:58,760 --> 00:19:05,700
the cable broke. Both ships returned, respliced, and once again parted ways.

233
00:19:06,520 --> 00:19:10,120
Three or four times they do this, and the last time they've actually gotten a fair distance,

234
00:19:10,500 --> 00:19:17,340
and it breaks, and they've agreed that if they get more than maybe 200 miles of cable

235
00:19:17,341 --> 00:19:20,580
and paid out, then they wouldn't have any left over to complete the Enterprise,

236
00:19:20,581 --> 00:19:22,260
and then they just take off for home.

237
00:19:22,740 --> 00:19:24,880
Well, it's very close to that amount.

238
00:19:24,980 --> 00:19:26,280
In fact, they've reached that limit.

239
00:19:27,640 --> 00:19:30,500
The ship's headed back to Ireland to restock

240
00:19:30,501 --> 00:19:34,440
in hopes that Field could convince the board of directors for a fourth try.

241
00:19:35,320 --> 00:19:37,600
Several board members resigned in disgust,

242
00:19:37,720 --> 00:19:40,440
but once again, Field's persistence prevailed.

243
00:19:41,120 --> 00:19:44,180
Field, always the optimist, says, let's go again.

244
00:19:44,900 --> 00:19:48,220
A calculation is made about how much cable is left on board,

245
00:19:48,221 --> 00:19:52,920
And lo and behold, there is just enough to get across the Atlantic.

246
00:19:59,820 --> 00:20:05,140
July 29, 1858, the two ships were back in the middle of the Atlantic,

247
00:20:05,360 --> 00:20:07,880
splicing cable again for the fourth time.

248
00:20:08,540 --> 00:20:13,680
They were about to achieve both success and failure in the highest degree.

249
00:20:13,681 --> 00:20:22,200
amazingly within just two weeks time both ships having successfully laid over a

250
00:20:22,201 --> 00:20:27,560
thousand miles of cable each finally reached the coasts of Newfoundland and Ireland

251
00:20:27,561 --> 00:20:38,220
now the first time in history North America in Europe were linked in Britain had instant

252
00:20:38,221 --> 00:20:43,141
communication with the colonies she had lost 75 years earlier.

253
00:20:43,142 --> 00:20:45,840
This was an epic moment in history

254
00:20:45,841 --> 00:20:55,529
and the world knew it. Overnight from being a crowd of lunatics they became heroes.

255
00:20:55,530 --> 00:20:56,580
Field was

256
00:20:56,581 --> 00:20:58,521
called Field the Great.

257
00:20:58,522 --> 00:21:05,020
The Queen Victoria knighted two of the British contingent and there was great

258
00:21:05,021 --> 00:21:10,400
rejoicing in the streets of New York. Fireworks, much eating, much drinking.

259
00:21:10,401 --> 00:21:16,900
They had parades up Fifth Avenue. Sunday there were pronouncements from pulpits in

260
00:21:16,901 --> 00:21:22,480
New York and Boston and Syracuse. This was bonding the two English-speaking

261
00:21:22,481 --> 00:21:28,660
worlds together. It was bonding the Christian nations together and the

262
00:21:28,661 --> 00:21:34,180
cables were seen as spreading word about our economic systems, our Christian

263
00:21:34,181 --> 00:21:39,896
heritage. The celebrations were comparable to the ending of a war.

264
00:21:39,897 --> 00:21:42,840
On August 16, 1858, Queen Victoria

265
00:21:42,841 --> 00:21:50,080
sent a congratulatory 96-word message to President Buchanan that prompted even more celebrations.

266
00:21:50,780 --> 00:21:56,060
The amount of fireworks filling the skies caused New York City Hall's tower to burn down,

267
00:21:56,620 --> 00:21:58,842
almost taking the entire building with it.

268
00:21:58,843 --> 00:22:01,540
But what the public didn't know was that there were

269
00:22:01,541 --> 00:22:05,560
already problems with the new cable, serious problems.

270
00:22:07,160 --> 00:22:09,440
The public went wild.

271
00:22:09,960 --> 00:22:15,320
This kind of concerned Field, because he knew the cable wasn't perfect, they were having

272
00:22:15,321 --> 00:22:18,060
problems, messages were coming across garbled.

273
00:22:18,560 --> 00:22:23,400
Although they were coming across, it would take an hour to get a word across.

274
00:22:24,220 --> 00:22:30,320
Meanwhile, all this pandemonium's breaking out, and Field, I think, became more and more

275
00:22:30,321 --> 00:22:36,800
concerned about the health of the cable and ultimately during the peak of

276
00:22:36,801 --> 00:22:45,020
celebrations the cable stopped working. One day short of a month the line went

277
00:22:45,021 --> 00:22:54,600
dead. Public reaction quickly turned. Field was considered by many a master

278
00:22:54,601 --> 00:22:58,840
crook. There were some who doubted that the cable had been late at all. One

279
00:22:58,841 --> 00:23:04,060
newspaper reporter proved that it hadn't. Now Western Union's plan seemed

280
00:23:04,061 --> 00:23:11,340
confirmed as the correct one. The cable of 1858 failed in part because the

281
00:23:11,341 --> 00:23:17,320
electrical engineer of the project was of the opinion that the bigger the

282
00:23:17,321 --> 00:23:23,740
voltage the better. Ran a current of 2,000 volts through the line and as a

283
00:23:23,741 --> 00:23:27,960
As a result, he basically fried the cable.

284
00:23:27,961 --> 00:23:30,820
Just what went wrong is a matter of conjecture.

285
00:23:30,821 --> 00:23:35,980
Most likely it was a 2,000 volt current that the engineer sent pulsing through the line.

286
00:23:35,981 --> 00:23:40,760
But several other reasons for the failure have been suggested also.

287
00:23:40,761 --> 00:23:45,760
All of them related to quality control, to an understanding of electricity, to an understanding

288
00:23:45,761 --> 00:23:48,440
of the manufacturing process.

289
00:23:48,441 --> 00:23:52,500
whatever the reason may be, it certainly failed.

290
00:23:53,580 --> 00:23:58,520
For Cyrus Field, organizing another attempt wouldn't be as easy as before.

291
00:23:59,340 --> 00:24:01,840
The United States was on the verge of a civil war,

292
00:24:01,980 --> 00:24:06,600
resulting in more pressing issues for potential investors and governments to worry about.

293
00:24:07,180 --> 00:24:12,420
It would be eight long years before America and Europe would speak to each other again

294
00:24:12,421 --> 00:24:14,460
across the bed of an ocean.

295
00:24:14,461 --> 00:24:22,460
after the failure of the cable field was vilified by the public many believed he was a charlatan

296
00:24:22,461 --> 00:24:27,380
acquaintances crossed the street to avoid him modern marvels will return

297
00:24:27,381 --> 00:24:39,220
by 1861 the united states was in full civil war all of fields u.s telegraph facilities were

298
00:24:39,221 --> 00:24:43,980
quickly put at the disposal of the union the united states government wanted to keep in

299
00:24:43,981 --> 00:24:47,980
constant communication with its Union forces from Washington, D.C.

300
00:24:48,780 --> 00:24:50,840
During the beginning of the Civil War,

301
00:24:51,180 --> 00:24:55,520
Field stayed in fairly close contact with Abraham Lincoln.

302
00:24:56,960 --> 00:24:59,180
Lincoln consulted with Field because,

303
00:24:59,181 --> 00:25:03,560
despite Field's failure with the 1858 expedition,

304
00:25:03,960 --> 00:25:06,600
he'd acquired an immense amount of knowledge

305
00:25:06,601 --> 00:25:12,400
regarding the practicalities of laying telegraphic cable along Bistons.

306
00:25:13,180 --> 00:25:15,320
Cyrus Field was extremely patriotic.

307
00:25:15,760 --> 00:25:18,480
In fact, a selfless patriot.

308
00:25:19,080 --> 00:25:20,680
He was a real supporter of the Union.

309
00:25:21,400 --> 00:25:26,020
At no cost to the government, he extended his telegraph service

310
00:25:26,021 --> 00:25:30,600
to the War Department and to the Navy Yard and other strategic points around the Capitol.

311
00:25:31,500 --> 00:25:34,880
His services proved invaluable to the Union.

312
00:25:35,600 --> 00:25:41,460
But during America's Civil War, Field kept his dream alive of a transatlantic cable.

313
00:25:42,180 --> 00:25:46,740
Traveling between America and England 31 times during the Four Year War,

314
00:25:47,080 --> 00:25:49,780
he was eventually successful in raising capital.

315
00:25:51,220 --> 00:25:55,020
British investors again rose to the challenge of backing such an attempt.

316
00:25:55,500 --> 00:25:59,360
Americans, on the other hand, were still partial to the advice of Western Union.

317
00:26:01,460 --> 00:26:04,120
Nevertheless, Field got his money.

318
00:26:04,720 --> 00:26:06,200
It was time to order cable.

319
00:26:06,780 --> 00:26:09,980
This time, instead of fabricating the wire in pieces,

320
00:26:09,981 --> 00:26:15,620
The entire cable, all 2,600 miles of it, was made in one piece.

321
00:26:16,280 --> 00:26:21,280
The manufacturers kept their factory going 24 hours a day, seven days a week,

322
00:26:21,580 --> 00:26:23,720
in order to complete the cable on schedule.

323
00:26:24,460 --> 00:26:28,900
It took eight months to make at approximately 14 miles per day.

324
00:26:29,520 --> 00:26:32,800
The finished cable weighed in at 9,000 tons.

325
00:26:33,760 --> 00:26:37,400
Of course, this one piece, 9,000 tons,

326
00:26:37,401 --> 00:26:40,060
meant that the factory had to be right on the waterfront,

327
00:26:40,340 --> 00:26:43,100
and so the cable was then pulled from the factory tanks

328
00:26:43,101 --> 00:26:48,380
over a line of pulleys and coiled into the tanks on the ship.

329
00:26:48,800 --> 00:26:50,980
Incidentally, this is exactly how it's done these days.

330
00:26:51,320 --> 00:26:52,560
Nothing has changed there.

331
00:26:53,960 --> 00:26:57,340
9,000 tons of cable was a heck of a lot of cable.

332
00:26:57,960 --> 00:27:01,600
There was only one ship in the world that was large enough to carry such a load,

333
00:27:01,920 --> 00:27:03,120
and that was Great Eastern.

334
00:27:03,121 --> 00:27:08,000
The Great Eastern was the world's largest steamship of its day.

335
00:27:08,001 --> 00:27:13,800
A ship so large it was unable to turn a profit and had bankrupted three previous owners.

336
00:27:14,240 --> 00:27:16,600
But for laying cable, it was perfect.

337
00:27:17,920 --> 00:27:19,620
So it was basically available.

338
00:27:20,100 --> 00:27:24,500
And that was added to the arsenal of the Atlantic cable entrepreneurs.

339
00:27:25,140 --> 00:27:28,220
And they gutted it and put cable tanks down the hold.

340
00:27:28,600 --> 00:27:31,700
And now the entire cable could be carried by this one ship.

341
00:27:33,120 --> 00:27:36,760
A crew of 500 men was needed to operate the ship,

342
00:27:37,020 --> 00:27:40,160
of which 200 were needed merely to raise its anchor.

343
00:27:42,420 --> 00:27:44,440
Supporting this crew and feeding it

344
00:27:44,441 --> 00:27:47,920
meant the Great Eastern carried a dozen oxen for deck work,

345
00:27:48,440 --> 00:27:51,480
plus 20 pigs for breeding meat while at sea,

346
00:27:51,900 --> 00:27:56,140
one cow, 120 sheep, and a whole poultry yard of fowl.

347
00:27:56,141 --> 00:28:01,080
When finally loaded, the Great Eastern weighed 21,000 tons.

348
00:28:03,120 --> 00:28:13,740
July 23rd, 1865.

349
00:28:14,200 --> 00:28:17,740
The Great Eastern set sail westward with little fanfare.

350
00:28:17,741 --> 00:28:22,060
No parties, no speeches, just serious business.

351
00:28:23,220 --> 00:28:28,880
They got about 80 miles out and a defect was noticed in the cable.

352
00:28:29,160 --> 00:28:32,380
It looked as though a pin had been embedded in the cable.

353
00:28:33,120 --> 00:28:37,920
They cut out the bad section, spliced it back together, continued on.

354
00:28:38,380 --> 00:28:42,260
Several days later, they noticed another pin in the cable.

355
00:28:43,160 --> 00:28:50,620
At that time, Field and others became suspicious of what was going on and suspected sabotage.

356
00:28:51,780 --> 00:28:57,620
Field was in a rage, and he promptly posted a 24-hour security watch over the cable tanks.

357
00:28:58,500 --> 00:29:01,280
Often, it was Field himself who stood guard.

358
00:29:01,281 --> 00:29:03,680
He was taking no chances.

359
00:29:05,080 --> 00:29:09,280
Beal was on board for every attempt to lay the Atlantic cable.

360
00:29:09,700 --> 00:29:12,120
He spent many nights on deck.

361
00:29:12,320 --> 00:29:14,780
He was just obsessed with the whole project.

362
00:29:15,060 --> 00:29:17,100
Nothing was going to get in the way of him.

363
00:29:17,880 --> 00:29:20,800
Sabotage, money, political problems.

364
00:29:22,540 --> 00:29:27,460
The possibility of sabotage would turn out to be only a minor setback.

365
00:29:27,461 --> 00:29:32,640
With only 600 miles of wire left to lay, disaster struck again.

366
00:29:32,641 --> 00:29:36,400
The cable fouled in the machinery and snapped.

367
00:29:37,380 --> 00:29:43,000
Whipping like a snake, the broken end of the cable plunged into the sea as the dismayed crew looked on.

368
00:29:43,920 --> 00:29:49,660
Five million dollars worth of cable, a quarter of a billion dollars in today's money,

369
00:29:50,580 --> 00:29:55,720
sunk into the Atlantic Ocean, into one of its deepest sections, and disappeared.

370
00:29:57,460 --> 00:30:00,640
With the future of international communications at stake,

371
00:30:01,100 --> 00:30:04,460
the crew struggled to grapple the cable and bring it to the surface.

372
00:30:06,160 --> 00:30:09,840
They turned the enormous ship perpendicular to the cable

373
00:30:09,841 --> 00:30:14,700
and zigzagged back and forth while dragging a grappling hook on the ocean floor.

374
00:30:15,300 --> 00:30:19,660
They spent nine days and nights grappling for the cable.

375
00:30:20,200 --> 00:30:23,980
They recovered it on occasions, but before they could hoist it to the surface

376
00:30:23,981 --> 00:30:26,640
and fasten it to the ship, it broke again.

377
00:30:26,641 --> 00:30:28,440
And this happened a number of times.

378
00:30:29,160 --> 00:30:32,520
And finally, they were running short of fuel and provisions,

379
00:30:32,860 --> 00:30:35,640
and the decision was made to return to Ireland.

380
00:30:36,780 --> 00:30:40,180
Western Union's plan to go around the world the long way,

381
00:30:40,460 --> 00:30:42,480
again, looked to be the best.

382
00:30:43,200 --> 00:30:47,060
After the failure of the 1865 expedition,

383
00:30:47,720 --> 00:30:50,600
Field was still enthusiastic about the project.

384
00:30:51,000 --> 00:30:53,220
He knew it could be done.

385
00:30:53,221 --> 00:30:58,080
his enthusiasm rubbed off on others around him

386
00:30:58,081 --> 00:31:00,860
and he eventually was able to drum up the capital

387
00:31:00,861 --> 00:31:06,100
to order another 2,500 miles of even better cable

388
00:31:06,101 --> 00:31:07,800
than previous expeditions.

389
00:31:08,940 --> 00:31:12,460
But Field didn't forget about the previous year's cable.

390
00:31:12,880 --> 00:31:14,200
With better grappling equipment,

391
00:31:14,360 --> 00:31:17,040
he knew it was possible to salvage the lost wire

392
00:31:17,041 --> 00:31:19,100
and complete that circuit too.

393
00:31:23,220 --> 00:31:33,960
On June 13, 1866, the 700-foot-long Great Eastern left Valencia Island,

394
00:31:34,700 --> 00:31:39,180
paying out the new cable all the way to heart's content Trinity Bay, Newfoundland,

395
00:31:39,540 --> 00:31:43,800
landing the shore end on July 27, without incident.

396
00:31:46,140 --> 00:31:49,980
One can only imagine the elation Cyrus Field must have felt.

397
00:31:49,981 --> 00:31:57,580
This was 12 long years in the making, four major attempts and millions of dollars.

398
00:31:58,560 --> 00:32:00,820
Finally, he had beaten the odds.

399
00:32:02,260 --> 00:32:07,100
On the banks of Newfoundland, greeting the great ship were hundreds of men, women, and children

400
00:32:07,101 --> 00:32:12,140
who helped bring in the shore end of the cable over a floating bridge of 40 fishing boats.

401
00:32:13,260 --> 00:32:17,780
From heart's content, Field immediately sent the following message to Ireland.

402
00:32:19,980 --> 00:32:22,200
We arrived here at 9 o'clock this morning.

403
00:32:22,520 --> 00:32:23,840
All well, thank God.

404
00:32:24,180 --> 00:32:27,660
The cable is laid and is in perfect working order.

405
00:32:30,520 --> 00:32:32,720
But Field's job was not over.

406
00:32:33,500 --> 00:32:37,740
There was still the lost cable of the previous year sitting on the bottom of the ocean.

407
00:32:38,420 --> 00:32:42,680
They ordered the ship back out to sea, 680 miles east.

408
00:32:43,460 --> 00:32:48,140
Several days later, the crew began grappling for the broken cable of 1865.

409
00:32:49,980 --> 00:32:56,140
they grappled for the lost cable 30 times before it was eventually found and lifted out of the water

410
00:32:56,141 --> 00:32:58,700
on september 1st 1866.

411
00:33:02,140 --> 00:33:06,860
the wire was then spliced and the rest of the previous year's cable paid out

412
00:33:06,861 --> 00:33:12,700
the great eastern once again landed in newfoundland completing a second parallel circuit across the

413
00:33:12,701 --> 00:33:13,640
the Atlantic Ocean.

414
00:33:16,900 --> 00:33:24,880
From that point on, with only, you know, occasional interruptions, this country, America, and

415
00:33:24,881 --> 00:33:29,340
Europe, England, and so forth, have been in constant electrical communication.

416
00:33:30,700 --> 00:33:36,800
With the news of Field's successful transatlantic telegraph cable, Western Union, the company

417
00:33:36,801 --> 00:33:43,130
many Americans thought to be infallible, finally accepted defeat.

418
00:33:43,131 --> 00:33:45,760
On February 27, 1867, after

419
00:33:45,761 --> 00:33:52,320
having its project stalled by Siberian winters, Western Union abandoned its attempt to reach

420
00:33:52,321 --> 00:33:59,400
Europe by way of Alaska and Siberia. Cyrus Field's great obsession for epic projects

421
00:33:59,401 --> 00:34:04,236
didn't end with the Transatlantic Cable. He was always ready for new challenges.

422
00:34:04,237 --> 00:34:04,937
In fact,

423
00:34:04,938 --> 00:34:06,660
he had a voracious appetite for them.

424
00:34:07,600 --> 00:34:12,080
He went on to help build the New York Elevated Railroad.

425
00:34:13,380 --> 00:34:18,360
Unfortunately, Field partnered up with the infamous Jay Gould and Russell Sage.

426
00:34:19,100 --> 00:34:24,620
Field thought that tariff for the railroad should be as low as possible

427
00:34:24,621 --> 00:34:29,540
so that everyone could use them, and ultimately that would be better business.

428
00:34:29,541 --> 00:34:37,540
Russell Sage and Jay Gould were of the school that you squeezed as much money as you could out of anyone.

429
00:34:39,540 --> 00:34:44,540
Field prevailed in terms of keeping their rates low on the railroad.

430
00:34:44,541 --> 00:34:51,540
Unfortunately, both Jay Gould and Russell Sage held it against him for the rest of their lives

431
00:34:51,541 --> 00:34:55,540
and they ultimately squeezed him on a stock transaction

432
00:34:55,541 --> 00:35:02,620
and Cyrus Field lost $5 million in one day and died a broke man.

433
00:35:04,620 --> 00:35:09,860
Field died on December 2nd, 1891, but his dream lived on.

434
00:35:10,360 --> 00:35:15,240
By 1915, there were no less than 15 transatlantic telegraph cables

435
00:35:15,241 --> 00:35:17,500
stretching across the floor of the Atlantic.

436
00:35:18,260 --> 00:35:23,180
While the cables performed flawlessly, there was one major drawback.

437
00:35:23,181 --> 00:35:30,700
Because of the enormous length of the cable, the wires couldn't handle the complex and high-speed impulses of speech,

438
00:35:31,100 --> 00:35:38,380
which now dominated the majority of land telegraph cables thanks to Alexander Graham Bell's invention, the telephone.

439
00:35:39,420 --> 00:35:45,640
After 40 years of faithful service, the telegraph and its cables were now in trouble.

440
00:35:45,641 --> 00:35:54,100
The price of a telegram to Europe in 1866 was $1.25 per word.

441
00:35:54,460 --> 00:35:58,080
Today, allowing for inflation, it is 60 times cheaper.

442
00:35:58,620 --> 00:36:00,420
Modern Marvels will be right back.

443
00:36:01,440 --> 00:36:05,580
We now return to the transatlantic cable on Modern Marvels.

444
00:36:08,360 --> 00:36:13,800
With the invention of the telephone in 1876 by Alexander Graham Bell,

445
00:36:13,801 --> 00:36:19,300
telegraphs move from the mainstay of global communications to a secondary role.

446
00:36:19,301 --> 00:36:25,300
But it would take some time before engineers could make telephones work over great distances,

447
00:36:25,301 --> 00:36:27,300
such as the Atlantic Ocean.

448
00:36:27,301 --> 00:36:32,300
A telegraph signal is just on or off pulses of electricity is so simple.

449
00:36:32,301 --> 00:36:35,300
A telephone signal is much more complex.

450
00:36:35,301 --> 00:36:41,300
It's a very complex electrical wave, the very complex waveform of your voice.

451
00:36:41,301 --> 00:36:51,080
So it requires a much greater information carrying capacity than was available in the deep sea telegraph cables.

452
00:36:52,080 --> 00:36:58,120
A telephone signal would need to be amplified in order to be discernible across long distances.

453
00:36:58,940 --> 00:37:07,800
By 1912, AT&T had perfected the vacuum tube, invented just six years prior, into a workable amplifier.

454
00:37:07,801 --> 00:37:14,840
As a result, by early 1915, the first transcontinental telephone line opened,

455
00:37:15,320 --> 00:37:18,660
connecting the east and west coasts of the United States.

456
00:37:21,200 --> 00:37:24,220
Unfortunately for the submarine cable industry,

457
00:37:24,520 --> 00:37:30,840
vacuum tubes also enabled the newly invented radio to amplify its signals great distances too.

458
00:37:30,841 --> 00:37:37,620
In the 1920s, radio technology comes in, and with that, almost overnight,

459
00:37:37,800 --> 00:37:44,140
The cable industry takes a nosedive because the wireless technology is so much cheaper

460
00:37:44,141 --> 00:37:48,800
that for 90% of the time, they can carry messages instead of the cables

461
00:37:48,801 --> 00:37:50,980
and do it, as I say, much more cheaply.

462
00:37:51,920 --> 00:37:57,840
So the first route for transatlantic telephone service was radio telephone.

463
00:37:57,841 --> 00:38:01,580
Again, you're using vacuum tube amplifiers to amplify the radio signal.

464
00:38:02,440 --> 00:38:10,660
Radio telephone service opened up between the United States and initially England and then the rest of Europe in 1927.

465
00:38:11,580 --> 00:38:16,040
Initially, the capacity was one, one call at a time.

466
00:38:16,460 --> 00:38:22,360
So you'd place your name in the queue, and when you got to the head of the line, the operator would call you and say, it's your turn.

467
00:38:23,760 --> 00:38:28,440
However, wireless telephone service soon proved itself to be unreliable.

468
00:38:29,160 --> 00:38:33,260
With radio, callers are at the mercy of the atmosphere and weather conditions,

469
00:38:33,360 --> 00:38:35,220
which are neither simple nor stable.

470
00:38:35,940 --> 00:38:40,160
A more reliable means of transatlantic telephone communications was needed.

471
00:38:40,740 --> 00:38:43,700
Once again, business look to the submarine cable.

472
00:38:50,800 --> 00:38:57,740
It wasn't until the early 1950s that the vacuum tube amplifier had been perfected for deep sea use.

473
00:38:58,440 --> 00:39:09,760
In 1955, America's AT&T and the British Post Office began laying the first amplified telephone

474
00:39:09,761 --> 00:39:14,840
cable onto the floor of the Atlantic Ocean.

475
00:39:14,841 --> 00:39:20,400
Early transatlantic telephone communications required two cables across the ocean.

476
00:39:20,401 --> 00:39:24,560
One handled the west to east portion of the conversation, the other handled the east

477
00:39:24,561 --> 00:39:26,080
to west portion.

478
00:39:26,081 --> 00:39:31,520
It would take two years to lay the cable, a year for each direction.

479
00:39:31,521 --> 00:39:35,940
Thanks to the experience gained from past submarine cable ventures dating back to the

480
00:39:35,941 --> 00:39:41,200
days of Cyrus Field, most of the kinks and problems laying wire into the depths of the

481
00:39:41,201 --> 00:39:43,320
Atlantic were now solved.

482
00:39:43,321 --> 00:39:46,840
It was smooth sailing for two summers.

483
00:39:46,841 --> 00:39:53,700
On August 14, 1956, 90 years and 18 days after the Great Eastern had landed the end of the

484
00:39:53,701 --> 00:39:56,700
successful telegraph cable in the same waters.

485
00:39:56,701 --> 00:39:59,700
Workers made the final splice in the telephone

486
00:39:59,701 --> 00:40:04,560
cable known as TAT1, Transatlantic Telephone Cable 1.

487
00:40:10,000 --> 00:40:15,350
On September 25, 1956, the chairman of the board of AT&T, Mr.

488
00:40:15,351 --> 00:40:18,420
Cleo Craig, picked up the phone and

489
00:40:18,421 --> 00:40:21,311
called Her Majesty's Postmaster General, Dr.

490
00:40:21,312 --> 00:40:24,860
Charles Hill, on the other side of the Atlantic Ocean.

491
00:40:25,560 --> 00:40:28,920
This is Dr. Hill in London. Is that you, Mr. Craig?

492
00:40:29,100 --> 00:40:30,500
This is Mr. Craig.

493
00:40:30,660 --> 00:40:35,180
Well, it's very good to hear your voice and to join with you and our friends in Canada

494
00:40:35,181 --> 00:40:40,420
in inaugurating this, the first transatlantic telephone cable service.

495
00:40:41,620 --> 00:40:47,600
Within a few hours, one of the 20th century's greatest technical achievements passed quietly

496
00:40:47,601 --> 00:40:49,400
into everyday use.

497
00:40:50,780 --> 00:40:54,840
After the success of TAT-1 in 1956,

498
00:40:57,120 --> 00:41:01,100
cables were laid not just across the Atlantic,

499
00:41:01,620 --> 00:41:03,540
but in almost every other direction.

500
00:41:03,541 --> 00:41:06,420
And in the years after that,

501
00:41:06,840 --> 00:41:10,820
cables have been laid connecting every continent on Earth.

502
00:41:17,600 --> 00:41:27,680
By the mid-1970s, copper cables, which carry electrical impulses in waves, began to be

503
00:41:27,681 --> 00:41:30,980
replaced by fiber optic wires.

504
00:41:30,981 --> 00:41:37,260
These cables use extremely fine, flexible glass or plastic fibers, which transmit light

505
00:41:37,261 --> 00:41:40,980
pulses instead of electricity.

506
00:41:40,981 --> 00:41:46,560
With modern fiber optic technology, great distances for submarine cables are no longer

507
00:41:46,561 --> 00:41:47,713
an obstacle.

508
00:41:47,714 --> 00:41:55,400
On June 17, 1997, a cable project named FLAG, fiber optic link around the globe,

509
00:41:55,500 --> 00:42:02,020
was completed. This $1.2 billion cable lies at the bottom of 12 different oceans,

510
00:42:02,700 --> 00:42:09,900
crosses three continents, touches 12 countries, and is able to carry 600,000 simultaneous

511
00:42:09,901 --> 00:42:16,660
telephone calls per section. This cable touches 75% of the world's population.

512
00:42:18,900 --> 00:42:26,234
The flag cable is the longest submarine cable that has been built today.

513
00:42:26,235 --> 00:42:27,660
It is a 28,000

514
00:42:27,661 --> 00:42:36,280
kilometers system that extends from Port Curnow in the UK to Miura at the south end of Tokyo Bay

515
00:42:36,281 --> 00:42:36,981
in Japan.

516
00:42:36,982 --> 00:42:42,040
This cable provides considerably more bandwidth than has been provided in this same

517
00:42:42,041 --> 00:42:49,660
region, and it provides a synchronous, low error rate performance, better by far than satellites.

518
00:42:50,960 --> 00:42:57,420
In addition to being extremely expensive, a satellite call must travel 27,000 miles from

519
00:42:57,421 --> 00:43:02,085
Earth to the satellite and then another 27,000 miles back.

520
00:43:02,086 --> 00:43:04,980
A transatlantic cable call need only

521
00:43:04,981 --> 00:43:09,360
travel about 2,500 miles point to point.

522
00:43:09,361 --> 00:43:14,460
At the speed of light, this helps eliminate the annoying delays and echoes of a satellite

523
00:43:14,461 --> 00:43:16,260
telephone call.

524
00:43:16,261 --> 00:43:20,600
Furthermore, there's a problem of the security.

525
00:43:20,601 --> 00:43:23,340
The satellite signals are broadcast.

526
00:43:23,341 --> 00:43:29,700
Anybody can pick them up, whereas the cable is a point to point secure system.

527
00:43:29,701 --> 00:43:34,860
Thanks to fiber optic submarine cables such as Flagg, many countries have an affordable

528
00:43:34,861 --> 00:43:39,340
reliable means of communicating outside their country.

529
00:43:39,341 --> 00:43:44,820
Finally, virtually all parts of our world are connected.

530
00:43:44,821 --> 00:43:48,720
We can now fax a note around the world,

531
00:43:48,721 --> 00:43:51,900
watch our favorite movie on television,

532
00:43:51,901 --> 00:43:54,940
speak to someone 3,000 miles away,

533
00:43:54,941 --> 00:43:58,900
and download something off the internet, all simultaneously

534
00:43:58,901 --> 00:44:01,260
through one cable.

535
00:44:01,261 --> 00:44:05,220
There will be no limits on global communications,

536
00:44:05,221 --> 00:44:10,200
all because of the ability to sync cables to our ocean's floors.

537
00:44:10,201 --> 00:44:12,680
I think I'm a relatively young man, right?

538
00:44:12,681 --> 00:44:15,920
But when I started, I made telegraph cable.

539
00:44:15,921 --> 00:44:19,040
And now I'm involved in projects where we're talking about cables

540
00:44:19,041 --> 00:44:24,520
with capacities of 5 million telephone circuits,

541
00:44:24,521 --> 00:44:30,580
or a simple cable less than one inch diameter lying on the seabed.

542
00:44:30,581 --> 00:44:34,360
And this across any of the oceans of the world.

543
00:44:34,361 --> 00:44:38,360
It is impossible to see the full size of the market,

544
00:44:38,361 --> 00:44:41,880
but it obviously is going to be huge

545
00:44:41,881 --> 00:44:44,260
beyond the cognition of anybody.

546
00:44:44,261 --> 00:44:45,940
Huge beyond our imagination.

547
00:44:51,520 --> 00:44:53,480
Run again and come.

548
00:44:53,481 --> 00:44:54,980
Are you prepared?

549
00:44:54,981 --> 00:44:55,900
The end is now.

